              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %strong arithmetic pretopos
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "natural+numbers+object#in_a_symmetric_monoidal_category"} parameterized natural numbers object
            %strong list-arithmetic pretopos
            is a pretopos with all
            %em parameterized list objects
            (
            %a{:href => "#Maietti10"}> Maietti 10, 2.6
            ).
          %p
            Using the (equivalent) definition given in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Cockett90"} Cockett 1990
            %strong parameterized list object
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-type"} W-type
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+functor"} polynomial functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B+ A\times(-)\colon C\to C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+coproducts"} disjoint coproducts
            (here denoted “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            ”).
          %p
            Discussion via its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            = succeed "\u2026" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Maietti05"} Maietti 05
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Maietti10"} Maietti 10, p.6
          %p
            Maietti (
            %a{:href => "#Maetti05"}> 05
            ,
            %a{:href => "#Maetti10"}> 10
            ) proposed that list-arithmetic pretoposes serve as the
            %strong arithmetic universes
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"} André Joyal
            (cf.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Joyal05"} Joyal 05
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/incompleteness+theorems"} incompleteness theorems
            (cf.
            = succeed ");" do
              %a{:href => "#VanDijkOldenziel"} van Dijk/Oldenziel 2020
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#MaiettiVickers12"} Maietti-Vickers 2012
          %h2#firstorder_peano_arithmetic First-order Peano arithmetic
          %p
            In classical logic,
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic#firstorder_peano_arithmetic"} first-order Peano arithmetic
            is often called “PA”. Unlike
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic#secondorder_peano_arithmetic"} second-order Peano arithmetic
          %blockquote
            %p Let me expand on why the initial Heyting/Boolean pretopos with a parametrized NNO is equivalent to first-order intuitionistic/classical PA. [By intuitionistic PA, I mean the same thing as what is standardly called Heyting Arithmetic.]
            %p
              Remember, intuitionistic/classical PA is the first-order intuitionistic/classical theory with a sort
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 : N
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo :
                      %mi N
                      %mo →
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S : N \to N
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo ×
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} + : N \times N \to N
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ×
                      %mo :
                      %mi N
                      %mo ×
                      %mi N
                      %mo →
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times : N \times N \to N
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is injective,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is not in the range of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x + 0 = x
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x + S(y) = S(x + y)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \times 0 = 0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ×
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo ×
                      %mi y
                      %mo +
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \times S(y) = x \times y + x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              which contains
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              and is closed under
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              must contain all of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %p
              So, let’s see that a Heyting/Boolean pretopos with parameterized NNO proves everything first-order intuitionistic/classical PA proves: An NNO is an initial algebra for the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ↦
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \mapsto 1 + T
              endofunctor, and thus by Lambek’s lemma, we obtain an isomorphism between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 + N
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              is the carrier of the NNO and the isomorphism is given by the combination of zero and successor. This being an isomorphism says in the internal language of the pretopos that every element of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              is precisely one of zero or a successor (not both; thus,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is not in the range of the successor map) and that the successor map is injective.
            %p
              Furthermore, our recurrence identities for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ×
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
              directly describe how to define unique operators satisfying those recurrences on a parametrized NNO.
            %p
              Finally, note that Heyting pretoposes are “closed under” first-order definability, so to speak (this is their raison d’être as a concept), so that any first-order definable relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(n, x)
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              and some other tuples of sorts
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              in this language will correspond to some subobject of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \times X
              in this pretopos. The set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x : X \mid R(0, x) \wedge \forall n : N \left( R(n, x) \Rightarrow R(S(n), x) \right) \}
              is first-order definable as a subobject of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              in this pretopos, and by passing to the slice category over it, we obtain another pretopos, where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              remains an NNO. In this slice pretopos,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{n : N \mid R(n, x) \}
              is a subobject of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              which contains
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              and is closed under
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              as algebras for the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ↦
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \mapsto 1 + T
              endofunctor. But as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              is an initial algebra, it is isomorphic to this subalgebra (any subobject of an initial object is isomorphic to the initial object). Thus, in this slice pretopos over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
